Viewing Logs (The most important for debugging!)
When something fails (like a generation job), you always want to check the logs.

View worker logs (GPU/Generation tasks):

bash
docker-compose logs -f worker
(The -f flag "follows" the logs live. Press Ctrl+C to exit).

View API logs (Web server & API requests):

bash
docker-compose logs -f api
View logs for all services at once:

bash
docker-compose logs -f
View only the last 100 lines of a log:

bash
docker-compose logs --tail=100 worker
2. Applying Configuration Changes
If you change .env or edit the Python code, you need to tell Docker to apply those changes.

Apply .env changes (Forces containers to recreate with new environment variables):

bash
docker-compose up -d --force-recreate
Apply Code changes (Rebuilds the Docker images and restarts containers):

bash
docker-compose up -d --build
Just restart a container (If it's stuck or you just want to reboot it without changing configurations):

bash
docker-compose restart api worker
3. Managing the System State
Start everything in the background:

bash
docker-compose up -d
Stop everything safely:

bash
docker-compose down
Stop everything AND wipe the database / clear Redis queues (Warning: This deletes all your jobs and data!):

bash
docker-compose down -v
4. Running Commands Inside the Containers
Sometimes you need to run a manual script or check a file from the perspective of the application.

Open a bash shell inside the API container:

bash
docker exec -it alphanord-api bash
Check what's inside the storage folder (where videos are saved):

bash
docker exec -it alphanord-worker ls -la /home/alphanord/app/storage/videos



